Persistent cloud lighting volume candidate
==========================================

Authority and scope
  Source: docs/PBR.CloudLightingVolume.Proposal.md, complete proposal.
  User direction: implement this plan only through explicitly requested phases. Phase 1 is specification
    work; runtime implementation and final qualification belong to subsequent phases.
  Context: docs/PBR.Clouds.md, docs/PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md and docs/PBR.CloudRealtime.todo.
  Paths below are repository-root relative unless explicitly stated otherwise.
  This plan owns a removable candidate, not a replacement of the existing renderer or completion
    of its realtime checklist. The existing renderer remains the default during evaluation.
  Retain native weather and finite 50-world-unit column support, physical units, connected cuboid
    faces, placement/wind anchoring, both PBR modes and the native six-attachment OIT contract.
  The candidate stores lighting in a shallow 3D grid. It still evaluates native density and integrates
    the current view. Retained illumination is approximate and may be older than the current frame.
  Non-goals: complete persistent density storage, sparse bricks, clipmaps, multiple grid levels,
    lighting mip chains, slice/cell rasterization alternatives, screen-space temporal accumulation,
    replacement weather, external cast-shadow publication, a generic rendering framework or migration.
  The selected initial view is full resolution without spatial resolve; adding reduced-resolution
    reconstruction requires a later decision. Full density caching requires a separate decision.

Execution and evidence
  Keep candidate runtime under VanillaGraphicsExpanded/PBR/Clouds/LightingVolume/ and shaders under
    VanillaGraphicsExpanded/assets/vanillagraphicsexpanded/shaders/cloud_lighting_volume/.
  Candidate -> native/public scene contracts and general rendering infrastructure is the dependency
    direction. Existing cloud algorithms must not depend on candidate resources or shader branches.
  Use actual GPU/program/binding/submission/barrier abstractions, including in focused test fixtures.
  Keep composition roots thin, files responsibility-focused, classes/methods documented and visibility
    regions consistent with repository conventions. Permanent architecture docs describe delivered code.
  Build/test execution goes through subagents. Each implementation phase uses cached incremental
    builds and affected contract checks only. Reuse unchanged passing evidence; expand checks only for
    concrete changes, failures or evidence gaps. No routine clean/full-catalog rebuild, full baseline
    reproduction or packaging gate. An unexpected full-catalog rebuild is an issue to investigate.
  Full comparison matrices and canonical packaging occur once against the frozen candidate in Phase 8.
  Automated tests may enforce declared arithmetic, physical units, bounds, identities, lifecycle and
    work limits. They must not enforce approximation accuracy, convergence, image similarity, noise
    reduction or visual fidelity. Do not create redundant GPU suites or increase budgets to satisfy
    approximation thresholds. Diagnostics report approximation differences without pass/fail gates.
  Separate source review, mathematical/GPU correctness, controlled timing, packaging and user-run
    appearance evidence. Never launch or close the game. Report contention during measurements.
  Before marking a phase complete, reconcile its source requirements, review its diff and record
    focused evidence and remaining limitations. Later phases and live acceptance remain unchecked.

Selected specification
  Authority: proposal / Selected candidate contract. Its constants are initial experiment inputs,
    not measured production defaults, an FPS target or acceptable approximation-error thresholds.
  Grid: 100x25x100 world-unit cells, stable vertical band [-400,400], finite native horizontal extent,
    fixed ceil(W/2)+1 horizontal interior cells and guard nodes, at most 103x34x103 texels.
  Storage: RG16F direct transmission, two RGBA16F fields with scaled diffuse RGB and unscaled alpha masks, descriptor and region
    circular history and region-remap storage. Maximum owned payload 10,694,704 bytes (10.20 MiB); ceiling 32 MiB.
  Transport: albedo .9, HG g=.6; direct 32 uniform midpoints / 1500 units; diffuse six axis directions,
    twelve uniform midpoints / 300 units and explicit isotropic environment boundary. The retained
    diffuse term includes transported environment, which view composition must not add twice.
  Scheduling: 512 direct region-source jobs and 256 diffuse region jobs per eligible frame, half
    reserved for round-robin fairness; fixed-domain worst-case revisit 43 eligible frames. Dirty age
    and backlog remain observable. Compatible changes retain stale lighting; hard local changes
    invalidate filter footprints. Nonlocal demand conservatively marks the domain dirty.
  Publication: independent read/write fields, complete RGBA copy before partial updates,
    lighting/region remap spares, circular descriptor addressing and absolute region keys. Initialized fallback switches immediately to
    completed compatible lighting. Candidate radiance bounds include lifetime retained maxima.
  View: full resolution, deterministic midpoint budget capped at 96 / distance 10000, stop .005,
    no temporal accumulation or spatial resolve. Exposure/OIT/depth/atmosphere remain shared contracts.
  Interface: selected consumer preparation, reset/disposal and radiance-bound admission only.
    Keep renderer default Existing. Actual format support, appearance and performance remain unverified
    until their implementation/qualification tasks; changing constants requires an explicit recorded edit.

Phase 1: Specify finite algorithms and candidate contracts
  Outcome: later owners can be implemented without inventing storage, numerical or scheduling policy.
  Validation: source inspection and arithmetic/design review; no baseline matrix or packaging.
  ☑ Reinspect native preparation/publication, scene-lighting admission, depth demand and OIT contracts.
    - Locate the narrow selection seam in CloudNativeRuntime and establish the safe ownership-switch
      boundary. Record whether a minimal preparation/reset/disposal renderer interface is necessary.
    - List allowed shared camera, atmosphere, calibration, encoding and GPU helpers. No dependency
      on CloudVolumeReconstruction, CloudShadowCache or current cloud transport include graphs.
  ☑ Specify material/world/grid transforms, admitted finite domain, vertical margin and region identity.
    - Resolve fractional wind exactly once, integer recentering, whole-cell shifts, newly exposed slabs,
      large displacements and density outside lighting coverage. Define thin-feature fallback admission.
  ☑ Select initial dimensions, formats and resource ceilings from explicit byte accounting.
    - Account for direct channels, both diffuse iteration buffers, descriptor history, metadata,
      initialization/copy storage and view targets. Record supported-limit checks and allocation failure.
    - Resolve filtering footprints and border validity without manual eight-fetch trilinear filtering.
  ☑ Specify direct integration and diffuse transport with explicit units, directions, weights and steps.
    - Define directional first-scattering seed, higher-order source units, extinction/albedo factors,
      environment boundary and final composition without double counting first scattering.
    - Normalize nonnegative angular weights; empty space carries light but adds no scattering.
      Define bounded ray support and iteration read/write semantics; do not substitute repeated blur.
  ☑ Resolve scheduling, refresh/invalidation classification and source-transition policies.
    - Define work limits, nonstarvation, dependency influence, age/backlog and coherent partial publication.
    - Specify current unshadowed direct plus coarse environment fallback, zero higher-order fallback,
      and any local transition smoothing. Invalid prior-region data must never enter a transition.
    - Choose deterministic view sampling/resolution and disposition the optional spatial resolve.
  ☑ Completion: record decisions with rationale in the proposal/design record; all dependent open
    choices above are concrete. No speculative choice becomes an unstated requirement or new subsystem.

  Specification verification (2026-10-19)
    Task-to-source mapping: native/publication task -> proposal / Native admission and shared boundary,
      PBR.Clouds.md native/depth/OIT contracts, PBR.Atmosphere.Publication.md publication/physical units,
      Rendering.Enablement.md and inspected runtime/scene sources. Coordinate/storage tasks -> proposal /
      Coordinates, coverage and allocation plus coherent publication. Transport task -> Density, ray
      schedules and source units plus Physical bounds. Scheduling/view task -> Change classification
      and bounded scheduling plus Deterministic view and initial controls.
    Second review and independent audit verified these specifications and arithmetic. Review corrected
      descriptor/region remap storage and fixed grid dimensions under recentering. All six entries are
      satisfied as specification work; no runtime implementation, build, GPU or appearance claim is made.
    Existing CloudRealtime scope/test/execution policy was consulted; its renderer-specific transport
      and same-frame-cache requirements are not imposed on this separately authorized candidate.

Phase 2: Own the grid, resources and isolated density queries
  Depends on Phase 1. Outcome: a fixture can allocate, remap and sample initialized candidate storage.
  Validation: cached affected build and focused domain/resource/typed-binding checks; no packaging.
  ☑ Implement candidate domain/storage owners and private density parameters/includes.
    - Preserve native extinction conversion, finite support, connected faces, bounded detail and
      material anchoring. Permit narrowly duplicated density math without extracting a broad framework.
    - Register candidate shader roots and typed bindings through existing discovery/program owners.
  ☑ Allocate chosen lighting formats, coherent iteration storage and exact region metadata.
    - Pack occupied/solar-valid/lunar-valid/diffuse-valid bits as the exact integer 0..15 in alpha.
      Fetch masks with texelFetch; scale/filter RGB only. Keep domain identity in publication/region records.
    - Initialize all sampling footprints to defined fallback data; metadata is not interpolated.
      Use hardware trilinear filtering with explicit level selection and no cross-domain wrap leakage.
    - Bind each diffuse field to its exposure-independent power-of-two storage scale; decode before
      lighting calculations and clamp decoded physical values to Dcap. Publish scale and field together.
      Cover encoding/range and failed-publication contracts without approximation-quality assertions.
    - Account for total bytes and reject unsupported dimensions/formats before publishing readiness.
      Clean up partial allocation and allow lifecycle-controlled recovery.
  ☑ Implement overlap retention, new-slab initialization and full-domain replacement.
    - Preserve identity under camera motion, wind and integer recentering; revoke incompatible storage.
      Retain a stable vertical margin. View support must not be clipped to the lighting grid.
    - Define reset/retirement hooks; actual native lifecycle wiring follows in Phase 7.
  ☑ Completion: focused checks establish transforms, footprint validity, bounds, allocation cleanup
    and read/write separation. No existing cloud owner or shader requires candidate state.

  Implementation verification (2026-10-19)
    Domain/private density -> proposal / Coordinates, coverage and allocation; Density, ray schedules
      and source units; PBR.Clouds.md native-map/density contracts -> LightingVolumeDomain/Parameters,
      private density include and focused placement/support checks. No current transport imports.
    Storage/publication -> proposal / Stored quantities and filtering; Physical bounds and coherent
      publication -> typed maintenance program, LightingVolumeStorage/Encoding, target-specific
      format/limit admission, initialized guards, remap spares, exact masks and paired field scales.
    Retention/lifetime -> proposal / Coordinates and coherent publication; Rendering.Enablement.md
      lifecycle context -> overlapping key remaps, retained region timestamps, replacement retirement,
      reset/disposal and failed-publication recovery checks. Live wiring remains Phase 7 work.
    Second review and independent audit passed after replacing rounded-log scale admission with an
      exact significand check. Cached Release build passed; all five focused tests passed without skips
      (three CPU, two GPU). The two GPU checks totaled 556 ms. After the encoding-only fix, all three
      affected CPU checks passed again; unchanged GPU evidence was reused. Final shader caches reused
      464 production and 547 test variants with zero compiler invocations. Initial new-root registration
      invalidated catalog identities; subsequent builds reused them. No clean build, broad test matrix,
      packaging, live selection, appearance or runtime performance qualification was performed.
Phase 3: Observe changes and budget persistent work
  Depends on Phases 1–2. Outcome: current publications produce bounded, nonstarving update demand.
  Validation: focused scheduler/identity checks and minimal GPU descriptor-comparison coverage only.
  ☑ Observe current native input through matching synchronous publication certificates.
    - Keep observation and hard-change-reference descriptors in W-by-W circular textures keyed by
      positive-modulo material column coordinates. Retain overlapping slots without descriptor copies.
      Validate old slot identity against the last committed window/domain; initialize entering columns
      and enqueue departing influence before replacement. One writer per slot reads before updating;
      publish the window after barriers and revoke history after a failed in-place observation.
    - Compare support/extinction on the GPU using these exact descriptor records. Classify locally
      and atomically OR affected-region observation demand and the existing header refresh bit.
      Use the barrier-separated scheduler pass to merge demand while preserving pending ages;
      comparison never races on node masks or timestamps. Allocate no column-classification texture.
      Retain computed results and metadata, never a previous-frame native texture borrow.
    - Generation changes enqueue physically classified refresh/invalidation instead of clearing the
      entire volume. Missing current input withdraws submission without falsely publishing freshness.
  ☑ Implement region queues, work budgets, age tracking and the selected fairness rule.
    - Prioritize new regions, incompatible geometry, newly relevant sources, visible old regions and
      background refinement. Preserve backlog across publications and report actual revisit ages.
    - Broad changes may mark the domain dirty but may not cause an unbounded dispatch workload.
  ☑ Expand dependency updates beyond changed density cells.
    - Include down-light shadow influence and diffuse propagation support; carry resulting update
      demand through successive iterations. Do not label untouched dependent lighting as fresh.
    - Refresh direct visibility on direction changes and diffuse source on weather/lighting changes.
      Direct intensity-only changes retain transmission; mixed diffuse RGB is not rescaled as exact.
  ☑ Completion: authored change sequences demonstrate bounded work, retained backlog, nonstarvation,
    exact region identity and correct fallback admission. No convergence or image-quality assertions.

  Implementation verification (2026-10-19)
    Observation -> proposal / Change classification and bounded scheduling; PBR.Clouds.md borrowed-map
      contract -> LightingVolumeWork/Program and work_observation.glsl: current certified borrows,
      positive-modulo circular identities, cumulative hard references and atomic region demand.
    Scheduling -> proposal / Coordinates, coverage and allocation; Change classification and bounded
      scheduling -> work.csh/work_selection.glsl: stable remapped cursors, bounded GPU lists/priority
      heap, persistent pending ages, channel revisions and separate successful-batch completion.
    Dependencies/publication -> proposal / Physical bounds and coherent publication; atmospheric
      source units and Rendering.Enablement.md lifecycle boundaries -> barrier-separated demand,
      node-owned masks, global dependent refresh, source transitions, failed-work withdrawal and reset.
    Second review and independent completion audit passed. Cached Release build passed with no errors.
      All four affected GPU checks passed; after review fixes the two work checks passed again
      (590 ms observation/history/failure and 1074 ms maximum-domain fairness). Unchanged storage
      checks were reused. Tests cover retained backlog, negative/circular keys, cumulative changes,
      source activation/intensity/direction, frustum flags, failed publication, counter rollover and
      maximum-domain 43-frame fair selection. No approximation quality or convergence gate was added.
    Lists/header/heap use existing scratch; 2048 parameter bytes plus 63488 scratch bytes preserve
      the fixed 65536-byte allowance and 10.20 MiB maximum owned-payload accounting. No native map
      readback, new allocation class, lighting solver, live callback, full baseline matrix or packaging
      was added. Runtime cost, appearance, live lifecycle and debug-menu wiring remain later work.

Phase 4: Compute persistent direct-light transmission
  Depends on Phases 2–3. Outcome: selected regions retain independent solar/lunar transmission.
  Validation: incremental direct shader build and focused finite-integration/source-unit checks only.
  ☑ Implement deterministic bounded node-to-light density integration for each relevant source.
    - Store transmission separately from current illuminance/color. Preserve domain clipping and
      physical step lengths; no indefinitely accumulated jittered estimate across changing density.
    - Skip new work for zero-intensity sources; prioritize newly relevant sources and admit initialized
      fallback until matching direction/content data is available.
  ☑ Publish completed direct regions with barriers, source identity and update age.
    - Serialize solar/lunar and node-owned invalidation passes; preserve encoded diffuse RGB and other
      alpha bits during image-load/store. Barrier before any diffuse/view readers; no overlapping writers.
    - Neighbor filtering footprints remain initialized and compatible. Failed/incomplete work does
      not publish readiness. Visibility history follows the specified compatible-change policy.
  ☑ Completion: typed GPU fixtures establish source separation, declared quadrature, valid domains,
    finite bounded transmission and publication. Timing or physical-reference accuracy is not a gate.

  Implementation verification (2026-10-19)
    Direct integration -> proposal / Density, ray schedules and source units; Coordinates, coverage
      and allocation; PBR.Clouds.md native-map and atmospheric physical-unit contracts -> isolated
      LightingVolumeDirect/Program/bindings and direct.csh, private density and shared compiled options.
      Exactly 32 uniform midpoints, finite horizontal exit/1500-unit cap, metre weights and finite tau
      saturation; no main-view clip or optical early exit. Transmission excludes source illuminance.
    Publication -> proposal / Physical bounds and coherent publication; Change classification and
      bounded scheduling; Rendering.Enablement.md lifecycle boundary -> exact frozen native/source
      batch admission, serialized solar/lunar image writes, preserved diffuse RGB/unrelated masks,
      per-region arithmetic-failure bits and successful completion ages/revisions. No extra volume.
    Typed GPU checks cover both compiled detail variants, independent sources, intensity-only reuse,
      source normalization, finite support/gaps, malformed/extreme controls, guard validity, metadata,
      inactive-source skipping and post-write failure/recovery. Comparisons use the specified finite
      algorithm with float/half storage precision, never approximation-quality or dense-reference gates.
    Second review and independent audit passed. Validation corrected framebuffer visibility in the
      reusable test readback, activation demand that previously set priority without enqueuing work,
      and intermediate extinction overflow by protecting unit conversion before detail modulation.
      The existing scheduler regression now requires an actual selected job for an activated source.
    Final cached Release build passed with zero warnings/errors; only two production and four fixture
      variants rebuilt. Both direct GPU facts passed (1631 ms activation/failure, 648 ms quadrature/
      channel preservation); unchanged passing scheduler/observation facts were reused. Build/test
      receipts: artifacts/cloud-lighting-volume-phase4-build.log and -tests.log, with direct TRX under
      artifacts/test-results/. Shader registration initially invalidated catalog identities; subsequent
      checks reused caches. No clean build, broad baseline matrix, packaging or game run was requested.
    Candidate remains unregistered. Diffuse solving, view rendering, live integration, cost/appearance
      qualification and actual driver residency remain later work. Owned-payload accounting is unchanged.

Phase 5: Refine diffuse multiple scattering coherently
  Depends on Phases 2–4. Outcome: higher-order source is progressively updated without read/write races.
  Validation: incremental scattering shader build and focused transport/publication checks only.
  ☐ Implement the selected isotropic closure and bounded angular/ray integration.
    - Gather directional first scattering for each ray's outgoing direction plus published higher-order
      source. Include extinction, albedo, path lengths and the specified incoming environment boundary.
    - Preserve transport across empty gaps and finite domain boundaries. Do not wrap light transport
      or count the view's first-scattering response twice.
  ☐ Implement coherent partial-iteration publication using the mechanism chosen in Phase 1.
    - Read only published source; write distinct storage. Preserve untouched values when publishing.
      Re-encode retained texels in the existing copy pass when the required storage scale grows;
      encode new values with the same destination scale and retain the old field/scale on failure.
      Copy alpha exactly, including across remaps and RGB rescaling. Preserve direct/occupancy bits
      when publishing diffuse validity. Retain mask-bearing fields for direct-only operation.
      Account for RGBA copy traffic and additional direct/metadata read-modify-write traffic separately.
    - Consume scheduler budgets and carry age/dependency information forward. No dispatch observes
      a partially written neighbor as completed history; barriers use existing submission abstractions.
  ☐ Completion: focused arithmetic and publication checks cover normalized weights, source accounting,
    untouched-region retention and hazards. No iterative convergence or appearance threshold is added.

Phase 6: Draw the candidate through an independent view path
  Depends on Phases 2–5. Outcome: authored scenes render candidate illumination into native OIT products.
  Validation: cached view/resolve variants and focused clipping/composition checks; no full timing matrix.
  ☐ Implement the candidate renderer and deterministic view integrator using candidate settings.
    - Clip to current finite native support and foreground/liquid depths. Evaluate native density,
      cached direct/diffuse lighting and current view-dependent directional phase response.
    - Render thin support and out-of-lighting-domain density with the specified coarse fallback.
      Preserve sharp cuboid support independently of filtered illumination.
    - Apply current atmosphere/underwater, photometric encoding and all six OIT outputs through
      public contracts or narrowly scoped private adapters. Support both PBR selections.
  ☐ Implement the Phase 1 resolution decision and spatial resolve only if that decision requires it.
    - Use candidate-owned targets and explicit depth-aware reconstruction. Keep screen-space temporal
      accumulation absent; do not borrow current cloud reconstruction/history resources or settings.
  ☐ Compile out user-disabled shader paths and keep runtime branches for genuinely dynamic data.
  ☐ Completion: fixture rendering establishes physical units, declared sampling, support/depth clips,
    fallback and OIT/encoding arithmetic. Live selection remains unregistered until Phase 7 admission.

Phase 7: Integrate selection, lifecycle and focused diagnostics
  Depends on Phases 2–6. Outcome: users can select one removable candidate without coupling algorithms.
  Validation: incremental affected build and focused ownership/lifecycle checks; no release packaging.
  ☐ Wire the developer selector at CloudNativeRuntime volumetric preparation.
    - Add the minimal lifecycle interface only if required by Phase 1. Retire/unregister the old
      consumer before activating the selected one; never run both renderers in ordinary play.
    - Preserve default renderer, Off/Simple semantics, native weather ticking/hooks and change-driven
      enablement. Existing lighting admission/depth demand observes the selected owner generically.
    - Initial preparation failure preserves native ownership policy; an admitted draw failure withdraws
      publication without activating a second renderer halfway through the frame.
  ☐ Wire reset, context/world retirement, selection change and recovery to all candidate owners.
    - Dispose candidate storage, callbacks and targets. Resize retains compatible cloud-space history
      while recreating view targets; incompatible domain/layout/transport changes invalidate storage.
    - Pause leaves native weather advancement to its owner and follows the selected refinement policy.
      Restore the existing renderer's unchanged settings when returning to it; add no migration.
  ☐ Register minimal candidate settings and existing-menu debug visualizers.
    - Expose density support/lighting coverage, validity/fallback, age/backlog and transition behavior.
      Report update work and allocation counts without a new debug framework or redundant diagnostics.
  ☐ Completion: selection/recovery/lifecycle contracts pass focused checks and a dependency review
    confirms the candidate does not intertwine with current shadow, hierarchy, transport or history code.

Phase 8: Qualify cost, appearance and removability
  Depends on Phases 1–7. Outcome: evidence supports keeping, revising or removing the candidate.
  Validation: one frozen-candidate canonical build/package check and matched opt-in comparisons;
    reuse unchanged focused receipts and valid baseline provenance. Repeat only invalidated evidence.
  ☐ Freeze source/settings/shader identities; verify canonical production/test outputs and package assets.
    - Confirm candidate resources are packaged and default selection is unchanged. Cover both PBR
      modes, Off/Simple/Volumetric, failed preparation/recovery and retirement with existing receipts.
  ☐ Measure existing and candidate rendering with matched native inputs, extent and declared settings.
    - Separate descriptor observation, domain maintenance, direct/scattering updates, publication/copies,
      view integration, optional spatial resolve, CPU submission, total GPU cost and resident allocation.
    - Include cold initialization, stable warm views, camera/wind motion, evolving weather, low sun and
      source transitions. Report backlog, oldest lighting age and fallback coverage alongside timings.
    - State resolution/approximation differences and regressions. Warm-state savings alone do not
      qualify initialization spikes or sustained backlog. No preselected speedup/FPS claim is a gate.
  ☐ Obtain user-run appearance assessment of sharp support, thin clouds, gaps, grid motion, source
    transitions, stale lighting, edge artifacts and deterministic-view aliasing without temporal history.
    - Record latency/quality tradeoffs and the user's disposition; headless checks do not establish
      visual acceptance. Later temporal filtering, sparse storage or density caching require new scope.
  ☐ Audit removability and reconcile delivered documentation.
    - Enumerate exact candidate directories, settings/debug/shader registrations and the selection seam
      needed for removal. Confirm no current cloud shader branches or resource owners require them.
    - Verify return-to-existing behavior and complete candidate resource retirement. Do not delete the
      candidate merely to perform this audit or promote it to default without explicit user direction.
    - Keep PBR.Clouds.md current for delivered selection/ownership; keep existing realtime acceptance
      separate. Record decisions/deviations against the source proposal and unresolved qualification.
  ☐ Completion: correctness/package evidence, matched costs, user assessment and removal audit have
    an explicit keep/revise/remove disposition. Missing runtime evidence leaves qualification open.

Proposal coverage
  Status/purpose and relationship to current rendering -> scope, Phases 1, 6–8.
  Isolation/integration boundary -> execution policy, Phases 1–2, 6–8.
  Domain/resolution/geometry -> Phases 1–2, 6 and appearance qualification in Phase 8.
  Stored quantities/filtering -> Phases 1–2, 4–6 and allocation accounting in Phase 8.
  Direct illumination/progressive scattering -> Phases 1, 4–5, 6 source composition.
  Scheduling/changes/retained history -> Phases 1–3, 4–5 publication, Phase 7 lifecycle.
  View rendering/temporal behavior -> Phase 1 decisions, Phase 6, Phase 8 user assessment.
  Evaluation/removal criteria -> execution policy, Phases 7–8 and final acceptance below.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Every source requirement maps to delivered behavior or an explicitly agreed deviation/disposition.
  ☐ Candidate algorithms/resources remain isolated and removal requires only the enumerated boundary.
  ☐ Native ownership, physical scene/OIT contracts, both PBR modes and existing renderer selection work.
  ☐ Retained illumination has coherent publication, bounded work and observable validity/age/backlog.
  ☐ Focused correctness, canonical package, complete cost accounting and user appearance evidence exist.
  ☐ Remaining tradeoffs are explicit; neither production promotion nor unrelated realtime-task completion
    is inferred from this candidate's implementation or qualification.
